WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
The first film treated heritability as a pattern of variation across people.

2
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:10.600
If some variation tracks genotype, which DNA differences are involved?

3
00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:16.780
A genome-wide association study searches without choosing a candidate gene first.

4
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.820
Its answer also belongs to that study population and setting.

5
00:00:25.283 --> 00:00:29.943
Each person contributes a trait value and allele counts across the genome.

6
00:00:31.223 --> 00:00:36.363
At every marker, the study tests whether allele dosage is associated with the trait.

7
00:00:37.583 --> 00:00:42.623
The skyline is a computed plot of those tests, with taller points showing stronger

8
00:00:42.623 --> 00:00:48.223
evidence. Each point represents a statistical test, not a gene's importance.

9
00:00:49.383 --> 00:00:53.143
Testing many markers creates many chances for false positives.

10
00:00:54.563 --> 00:00:57.723
A stringent genome-wide threshold controls that burden.

11
00:00:59.223 --> 00:01:04.863
Ancestry differences between cases and controls can also create false signals, so

12
00:01:04.863 --> 00:01:07.803
principal components help model population structure.

13
00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:13.263
A threshold controls false positives; it does not remove confounding.

14
00:01:14.467 --> 00:01:17.607
A peak rarely identifies a causal base by itself.

15
00:01:19.087 --> 00:01:24.367
Nearby alleles can stay correlated for generations, because recombination separates

16
00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:29.567
them only slowly. That correlation is linkage disequilibrium.

17
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:35.827
A harmless marker may rise because it tags a causal variant in the same inherited

18
00:01:35.827 --> 00:01:36.827
block.

19
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.070
The tallest marker sits beside several correlated neighbors.

20
00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:44.490
Must that tallest point be the causal variant?

21
00:01:50.517 --> 00:01:56.937
No. Several variants correlated with the causal one can carry similar evidence.

22
00:01:58.497 --> 00:02:03.157
Fine-mapping uses the correlation pattern to rank variants and build a credible set,

23
00:02:03.677 --> 00:02:05.917
but that set still expresses uncertainty.

24
00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:10.967
Next ask which gene the variant affects in a relevant tissue.

25
00:02:12.447 --> 00:02:17.007
Colocalization tests whether trait and expression signals may share a variant.

26
00:02:18.247 --> 00:02:22.267
The target can be a neighboring gene, not the gene containing the marker.

27
00:02:23.417 --> 00:02:27.237
Editing then changes a candidate allele in the right cell type.

28
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:33.737
If gene expression and a relevant cellular phenotype change, the causal explanation

29
00:02:33.737 --> 00:02:39.197
gains support. A cell result still does not prove an effect in a person.

30
00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:44.190
Association estimates can also become a polygenic score.

31
00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:50.190
Each allele count is weighted by its estimated effect, then the terms are summed.

32
00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:55.730
The score must be tested in people who were not part of the original study.

33
00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.960
Carry those weights into a population with different linkage disequilibrium and

34
00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:02.960
allele frequencies.

35
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.720
The same tag markers may follow causal variants less closely.

36
00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.920
Prediction can weaken even when much underlying biology is shared.

37
00:03:13.983 --> 00:03:16.263
That matters before clinical use.

38
00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:22.343
Training data shape accuracy, and genetic prediction carries a history of coercion.

39
00:03:23.783 --> 00:03:27.063
Ask who was studied, who benefits, and who controls the data.

40
00:03:28.117 --> 00:03:30.197
Association narrows the search.

41
00:03:31.897 --> 00:03:34.257
Functional evidence in cells strengthens the case.

42
00:03:35.577 --> 00:03:39.817
Establishing a cause also needs whole organisms or human genetics.
